WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
In 2019, I put out a video about how two colliding blocks can compute pi.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.616
It's very fun, very surprising, and if you include the adaptations

00:00:07.616 --> 00:00:10.464
into shorts that I've posted since then, it is the most popular

00:00:10.464 --> 00:00:13.400
thing that I've put on the internet, and by a pretty wide margin.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.603
This year, for Pi Day, I want to revisit this topic,

00:00:16.603 --> 00:00:20.699
because there's actually a lot that I never talked about in the original video,

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:23.772
including a fact that I didn't know when I first posted it,

00:00:23.772 --> 00:00:26.127
and which I suspect no one in the world knew,

00:00:26.127 --> 00:00:29.507
which is how this is all secretly connected to quantum computing,

00:00:29.507 --> 00:00:33.040
more specifically, something known as Grover's algorithm for search.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.176
Also, the whole crowd pleaser is that this is connected to pi,

00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:41.640
but very technically speaking, that full connection to pi is an unsolved problem.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.740
I'll explain what I mean at the end here.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.211
Just to recap, in case you haven't seen it, the setup of that

00:00:47.211 --> 00:00:50.320
original video was to have two blocks on a frictionless plane.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.634
At the very beginning, the one on the left is stationary,

00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:57.739
and we'll think of it as smaller, maybe 1kg, and the block on the right is coming

00:00:57.739 --> 00:00:58.740
in with some speed.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:02.709
The two blocks bounce off of each other, there's a wall to their left,

00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:06.367
and the puzzle is to figure out how many total collisions would take place,

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:08.340
including the collisions with that wall.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.816
So for example, in the smallest case, where both blocks have the same mass,

00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:17.146
with each collision all of the momentum gets transferred from one block to the other,

00:01:17.146 --> 00:01:20.620
and including that one clack against the wall, the final count is 3.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.928
If that right block is bigger, maybe 100kg, it means it has a lot more momentum when

00:01:25.928 --> 00:01:30.640
it's coming in, so it takes a lot more collisions to fully redirect that momentum.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.820
In this case, with a mass ratio of 100 to 1, the total number of collisions is 31.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.630
For an even bigger mass ratio, let's say 10,000 to 1,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.473
that little block ends up getting really crammed up against the wall,

00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:47.920
and almost all of the collisions happen in this rapid burst right in the middle.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.569
In this case, it takes over 300 in total to turn that big block around,

00:01:55.569 --> 00:02:01.005
and after a nice long pause, as if the universe has a strong taste for drama,

00:02:01.005 --> 00:02:06.860
we see that the exact answer rolls in at 314, a number with eerily familiar digits.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.512
I do want to highlight a couple unrealistic things about this puzzle,

00:02:10.512 --> 00:02:12.580
the kind of nuances you can't fit into a short.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.762
When this big mass is growing, that burst of collisions

00:02:15.762 --> 00:02:18.260
in the middle just gets more and more concentrated.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.681
In order to get the delightful result here, we

00:02:20.681 --> 00:02:23.260
have to make a number of idealizing assumptions.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.500
One assumption is that no energy is lost in the collisions.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.720
Physicists would call those perfectly elastic collisions.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.553
The astute viewers would then be raising their hands and complaining,

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:36.120
well hang on, shouldn't that mean we don't hear any sounds?

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.471
This is true, I'm throwing in the sounds as an artistic choice,

00:02:39.471 --> 00:02:41.860
partly because I like it, it makes it kind of fun.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.944
But more substantively, I think it's otherwise really hard to communicate

00:02:45.944 --> 00:02:49.800
purely visually just how dense that burst of collisions in the middle is.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.786
Like here for example, with a mass ratio of a million to one,

00:02:53.786 --> 00:02:58.780
that burst contains almost 3,000 collisions, and the final count comes in at 3,141.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.743
Now as that big mass gets even more massive, that little block would have to move

00:03:03.743 --> 00:03:07.745
so quickly during that little burst that a physically accurate analysis should

00:03:07.745 --> 00:03:10.228
start to take into account relativistic effects,

00:03:10.228 --> 00:03:14.230
and there's just so many other practical realities that would cause everything

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.800
here to break down completely.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.589
But we're going to be ignoring all of that, we'll only be treating this as a pure,

00:03:20.589 --> 00:03:23.100
overly idealized classical physics puzzle.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.186
Because when you do, the big surprise is that as that large mass grows by powers of 100,

00:03:29.186 --> 00:03:33.460
the total number of collisions always has the same digits as pi.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.836
Now even if this gets unrealistic for big mass ratios,

00:03:37.836 --> 00:03:40.880
you can actually see it in practice for smaller ratios.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.859
This footage here for example comes from a group of students at the University of Bonne

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:50.260
demonstrating how when the mass ratio is 100 to 1, you really do get 31 collisions.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.958
Also Matt Parker just put up a video about the attempts that

00:03:53.958 --> 00:03:56.700
he and I and a couple others had to do so at Cambridge.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.948
What makes this puzzle great, aside from the surprising pi of course,

00:04:00.948 --> 00:04:04.140
is how well poised it is to be a general problem solving lesson.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.263
Very often when I present this as a lecture, I like to start by inviting the

00:04:08.263 --> 00:04:11.760
audience to begin by listing out a few general problem solving principles.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.239
You might enjoy thinking of a few of your own, and as we go through,

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:17.680
I'll highlight a couple that I have written down here.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.989
For example, one that's seemingly trivial but surprisingly useful is that if

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:25.814
you're stuck on a hard problem, and I would consider this a hard problem,

00:04:25.814 --> 00:04:30.260
it never hurts to just list any equations or theorems that might somehow be relevant.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.600
So for this puzzle where we have some colliding blocks,

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.706
two relevant laws that might come to mind, especially if you have a recent physics

00:04:37.706 --> 00:04:41.514
class somewhere in your history, would be the conservation of energy and the

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:42.800
conservation of momentum.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.553
The energy of one of these moving blocks would

00:04:46.553 --> 00:04:49.280
be ½ times its mass times its velocity squared.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.135
So let's say we call the mass of the big block m1 and call its velocity v1,

00:04:54.135 --> 00:04:59.117
and likewise the mass of that little block will be m2 and its ever-changing velocity

00:04:59.117 --> 00:04:59.820
will be v2.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.429
Then the total kinetic energy between these two blocks is what you

00:05:03.429 --> 00:05:06.580
get by plugging in the numbers and adding these two quantities.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.365
The conservation of energy essentially says this

00:05:09.365 --> 00:05:11.700
number won't change throughout the experiment.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.801
Those two velocities will change, but they have to do so in

00:05:14.801 --> 00:05:17.660
such a way that this whole expression equals the same number.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:22.300
This is all of course assuming that no energy is lost to friction or to the collisions.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.075
The other quantity that's conserved is momentum,

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:28.844
and the momentum for each block looks even simpler,

00:05:28.844 --> 00:05:30.920
it's just its mass times its velocity.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.908
So again, when you plug in the numbers and you compute this expression,

00:05:34.908 --> 00:05:38.713
you'll get something, and what the conservation of momentum tells us is that

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:43.160
whatever that number is, it doesn't change even after the blocks collide with each other.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.843
Now unlike energy, this momentum expression will actually change after

00:05:47.843 --> 00:05:51.082
that little block collides against the wall, because really what's

00:05:51.082 --> 00:05:54.320
going on is it's transferring some of its momentum into that wall.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.127
Technically speaking that should mean the wall starts moving a tiny amount,

00:05:58.127 --> 00:06:01.262
but again we'll be idealistic and assume the wall is supermassive or

00:06:01.262 --> 00:06:04.260
fixed into the earth, and any movement that it has is negligible.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.380
Another principle I have up here is to just draw pictures.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.820
When you're trying to figure something out, actually make some figures.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:15.130
Visual intuition can help to think about things in different ways,

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:18.000
and sometimes staring at the right diagram can expose a solution.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:22.837
So in this context, where the two values that are changing are v1 and v2,

00:06:22.837 --> 00:06:25.752
you might be inspired to draw a coordinate plane,

00:06:25.752 --> 00:06:31.000
where the x-coordinate encodes this value v1, and the y-coordinate encodes this value v2.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.890
Just to be crystal clear on what I mean by that,

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:38.480
as the experiment unfolds and the blocks collide with each other,

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.362
and their two velocities change with each collision,

00:06:41.362 --> 00:06:45.876
this little point inside our space is going to move around that space as those two

00:06:45.876 --> 00:06:50.607
velocities change, always doing so in such a way that its coordinates tell you what v1

00:06:50.607 --> 00:06:51.260
and v2 are.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.220
The hope is that as the whole experiment unfolds and we consider how exactly this

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.583
point bounces around this slightly abstract velocity space,

00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:04.123
studying the path of that point can yield insights about the underlying dynamics

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:05.020
for the blocks.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:09.647
Even though we're just getting warmed up, this really is the key step in our whole

00:07:09.647 --> 00:07:13.535
problem solving process, and it's a pretty common thing to do throughout physics,

00:07:13.535 --> 00:07:17.517
where if you have some dynamical situation with multiple numbers that are changing,

00:07:17.517 --> 00:07:21.594
when you package all those numbers together as a single point in a higher dimensional

00:07:21.594 --> 00:07:25.529
space, studying how that point moves through the space has a way of clarifying the

00:07:25.529 --> 00:07:26.240
whole problem.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:29.478
You could call this a state space, and this particular state

00:07:29.478 --> 00:07:32.415
space for us holds within it the key connection to the quantum

00:07:32.415 --> 00:07:35.120
computing parallel that we're going to be building up to.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.426
Now, in the context of this abstract velocity space,

00:07:38.426 --> 00:07:42.422
take a moment to think about what this conservation of energy equation

00:07:42.422 --> 00:07:43.660
is really telling us.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:47.692
When you take some number times x squared plus some number times y squared

00:07:47.692 --> 00:07:51.060
and set it equal to a constant, this is the equation for an ellipse.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.396
Our specific ellipse will be more squished in the x direction,

00:07:54.396 --> 00:07:59.185
since that number sitting in front of x is larger than the number sitting in front of y,

00:07:59.185 --> 00:08:03.220
and the size of the ellipse will depend on the total energy of the system.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:06.611
And this comes from what the initial velocity of that big block was,

00:08:06.611 --> 00:08:08.840
and interestingly, I never told you that velocity.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:10.900
It doesn't actually change the final answer.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:14.478
Whether it comes in fast or it comes in slow, you get the same total number

00:08:14.478 --> 00:08:17.760
of collisions, the experiment just unfolds more quickly or more slowly.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.266
As our experiment unfolds and that pair of velocities changes,

00:08:21.266 --> 00:08:26.299
it always does so in such a way that this point inside our state space stays constrained

00:08:26.299 --> 00:08:27.260
to this ellipse.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.680
If it landed anywhere else, it would mean energy was not conserved.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:34.804
Another principle I want to bring up here is how math and math-adjacent

00:08:34.804 --> 00:08:37.659
fields tend to reward you when you respect their symmetries.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.359
And in this case, we already know that pi is going to be somehow relevant to the answer,

00:08:42.359 --> 00:08:45.666
and pi has everything to do with circles, relating distances around

00:08:45.666 --> 00:08:48.000
the circumference of that circle to the radius.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:53.050
So on our hunt for pi, it's tantalizingly close to be working with an ellipse like this,

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.514
and you might feel like it would be all the more natural and maybe

00:08:56.514 --> 00:08:58.840
more promising if this was instead a circle.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:02.130
We can actually make this into a circle just by changing

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:04.720
what the coordinates of that state space represent.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.080
Here's what I mean by that.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.827
The equation for a circle looks like x2 plus y2 equals some constant,

00:09:09.827 --> 00:09:12.740
without any numbers sitting in front of those variables.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:18.340
If I let x not represent v1, but I let it represent the square root of m1 times v1,

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:22.840
then x2 gives us something that looks like 2 times the kinetic energy.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:26.825
And similarly, if y represents the square root of m2 times v2,

00:09:26.825 --> 00:09:31.360
that y-coordinate also more directly captures the energy of the little block.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.541
And this equation for the circle, x2 plus y2 equals some constant,

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:40.220
now captures the conservation of energy but in this rescaled coordinate system.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.337
Like I said, the hope is that adding a little more symmetry will make

00:09:43.337 --> 00:09:46.680
our problem easier to solve, and in a moment you'll see how this is true.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:50.620
Let's take a minute to get a little familiar with what this diagram is really telling us.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.868
For example, at the very beginning, that big block has a negative velocity,

00:09:54.868 --> 00:09:57.727
so we must be somewhere on this left half of the plane,

00:09:57.727 --> 00:10:01.760
and that little block starts off stationary, meaning the y-coordinate is zero,

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:05.129
and so we have to start out at the leftmost point of this circle,

00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:06.660
that's the initial condition.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:09.760
Now after they collide with each other, what happens?

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:10.680
Where do we end up?

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.035
Well, physical intuition tells us that that little block picks up some kind of negative

00:10:15.035 --> 00:10:18.979
velocity, so we're going to have to end up somewhere where the y-coordinate is negative,

00:10:18.979 --> 00:10:21.062
and that big block loses some of its momentum,

00:10:21.062 --> 00:10:24.563
so the x-coordinate is going to get a little closer to zero from where it was,

00:10:24.563 --> 00:10:26.380
even if you don't know exactly how much.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:28.800
And again, we have to stay somewhere on this circle.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.784
The circle represents all the points that have the same total energy,

00:10:32.784 --> 00:10:36.880
and so you might guess that our point lands somewhere on this arc right here.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.796
To be more exact, we need to introduce the second conservation law,

00:10:40.796 --> 00:10:42.380
the conservation of momentum.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.500
Keep in mind though, at this point I've changed what the coordinates mean.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.536
So in our new coordinates, the momentum of the first block looks like the square root

00:10:50.536 --> 00:10:55.460
of m1 times x, and the momentum of the second looks like the square root of m2 times y.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.804
It's all saying the same thing, it's just a different coordinate system,

00:10:58.804 --> 00:11:00.640
and those masses are just constants anyway.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.680
The significance is that this whole equation is a linear equation in x and y,

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:07.880
so in our diagram it's going to look like some kind of line.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:12.158
More specifically, you can work out it's a line whose slope is the

00:11:12.158 --> 00:11:15.900
negative square root of the big mass divided by the small mass.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.558
Now this slope is going to be very important for us,

00:11:18.558 --> 00:11:22.107
it's the key quantity that we'll use to get the numerical answer that has digits

00:11:22.107 --> 00:11:25.875
looking like pi, and that square root is going to be very important for us later when

00:11:25.875 --> 00:11:27.540
we get to the quantum computing case.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:29.180
So remember this expression.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.122
Where this momentum line sits left and right depends

00:11:32.122 --> 00:11:35.120
on what the total momentum of the pair of blocks is.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:38.393
We know that we start off on this leftmost point of the circle,

00:11:38.393 --> 00:11:40.260
so the line has to pass through that point.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:45.165
Every other point on this line is telling us every other pair of velocities that would

00:11:45.165 --> 00:11:48.308
have the same net momentum, and as you've no doubt noticed,

00:11:48.308 --> 00:11:51.189
this line intersects the circle at exactly two places,

00:11:51.189 --> 00:11:55.746
meaning there are only two pairs of velocities out there with this same kinetic energy

00:11:55.746 --> 00:11:58.260
and the same momentum as the initial condition.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:02.560
So after the blocks bounce off of each other, we must hop over to this other point.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:05.960
And honestly, that is most of the physical reasoning for this whole problem.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:08.562
Everything that's left is to just see how this

00:12:08.562 --> 00:12:10.960
unfolds and try to reason through what it implies.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.001
For example, that little block bounces off the wall,

00:12:14.001 --> 00:12:16.720
and all that does is flip the sign for the y-coordinate.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.443
It goes from being negative to positive, the x-coordinate

00:12:19.443 --> 00:12:22.100
doesn't change because nothing has influenced the big block.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:26.610
This has caused a change in the net momentum since some of it went into the wall,

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:30.023
so our momentum line has to move over to the right however far it

00:12:30.023 --> 00:12:32.920
needs to move so that it passes through this new point.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:35.526
And then when the blocks bounce off each other,

00:12:35.526 --> 00:12:39.411
again we look for the other place where that diagonal line intersects the circle,

00:12:39.411 --> 00:12:41.400
and you basically keep playing this game.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.502
Little block bounces off the wall and that moves you up,

00:12:44.502 --> 00:12:48.234
blocks bounce against each other, that moves you down and to the right,

00:12:48.234 --> 00:12:51.240
and you just keep going until… well, until what, exactly?

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:54.589
The experiment can't be done until both blocks are moving to the right,

00:12:54.589 --> 00:12:58.360
so you have to be in this quadrant here where the x and y coordinates are both positive.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:03.091
And also to be done, that little block has to be moving slower than the big block,

00:13:03.091 --> 00:13:06.380
meaning you sit somewhere below this line where v1 equals v2.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:09.849
Inside our new coordinate system, that's a line that has a

00:13:09.849 --> 00:13:12.331
slope equal to the square root of m2 over m1,

00:13:12.331 --> 00:13:16.540
a line perpendicular to those down and to the right ones we had been drawing.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.939
And let's go ahead and label this little green region the end zone,

00:13:19.939 --> 00:13:23.500
meaning when our state space point lands here, the whole experiment is done.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:28.749
With all of that, we've translated a physics question into a pure geometry question,

00:13:28.749 --> 00:13:31.300
one that's hopefully a lot easier to solve.

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:33.100
Our new puzzle is this.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:34.700
Imagine I said we have a circle.

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:37.480
You start at the leftmost point of that circle,

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:40.866
and your first move is to travel down and to the right along a line

00:13:40.866 --> 00:13:42.160
with some specific slope.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.700
Here I'm drawing a slope of 4, but it could be anything.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.369
And then you move straight up until you intersect the circle,

00:13:48.369 --> 00:13:52.152
and then down and to the right with that same slope, then straight up,

00:13:52.152 --> 00:13:56.200
down and to the right, over and over until you end up inside this end zone.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:58.560
The puzzle is, how many lines do you draw?

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:01.600
How many times do you zig and zag throughout this process?

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.340
Of course, the answer depends on the slope.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.500
If you have a steeper slope, you end up with more total lines.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.134
And the one connection we have to the original physics is that

00:14:11.134 --> 00:14:14.620
this slope comes from the negative square root of the mass ratio.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:18.320
For example, when the big mass is 100 and the little mass is 1,

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:22.960
that square root of the mass ratio looks like 10, so the slope is negative 10.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:28.120
And in fact we do get 31 lines, corresponding to the 31 collisions that those blocks have.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.017
It still might not be obvious how you count those lines,

00:14:31.017 --> 00:14:34.569
but it's certainly a lot less surprising that pi is relevant to the answer,

00:14:34.569 --> 00:14:37.980
and abstracted away from the physics, it is an easier question to solve.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:42.285
If you just stare at this diagram and you're the right combination of lucky and clever,

00:14:42.285 --> 00:14:43.880
here's something you might notice.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:47.278
Consider all of the points where these lines hit the circle,

00:14:47.278 --> 00:14:51.300
and consider all of the arcs along the circle in between those points.

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:55.065
You might notice those arcs seem to be about the same size,

00:14:55.065 --> 00:14:59.400
and you might hypothesize that all of these arcs are in fact exactly the same size.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.513
This does turn out to be true, and you can prove it using

00:15:02.513 --> 00:15:05.460
the ever useful and ever delightful inscribed angle theorem.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:08.755
For anybody who's a little bit rusty on this one,

00:15:08.755 --> 00:15:12.949
the setup is to have three points on a circle, which I'll call P1, P2,

00:15:12.949 --> 00:15:16.966
and P3, and we care about the angle between the line from P1 to P2,

00:15:16.966 --> 00:15:18.620
and the line from P2 to P3.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:22.193
It turns out, no matter where you place these points,

00:15:22.193 --> 00:15:27.320
that angle is exactly half of this angle right here, from P1 to the center to P3.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:31.532
It's really quite fun, you can imagine moving around these points however you want,

00:15:31.532 --> 00:15:34.960
you just have to make sure P2 never goes on that arc between P1 and P3.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.800
In our little circle puzzle, think about how this might be relevant.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:41.959
And maybe it helps if you focus on two different lines

00:15:41.959 --> 00:15:44.900
and the one arc of the circle connecting their endpoints.

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:48.953
These lines are separated by some angle, I'm going to call it theta,

00:15:48.953 --> 00:15:51.923
that angle is dependent just on the slope of the line,

00:15:51.923 --> 00:15:56.352
and using the inscribed angle theorem, we know that arc along the circle must run

00:15:56.352 --> 00:15:58.620
across an angle of exactly 2 times theta.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:02.180
Critically, it didn't matter which two lines I happened to highlight here.

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:05.963
In all cases, the angle between the vertical line and the ones down and to

00:16:05.963 --> 00:16:09.030
the right is the same, since the slope was always the same,

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:13.580
which means all of these little arcs along the circle cover an angle of exactly 2 theta.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:17.560
You might ask why this is helpful, how does this expose the number of lines?

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.060
And here's a way to think about it.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:23.134
As you play this game of bouncing down and to the right, going straight up,

00:16:23.134 --> 00:16:27.382
down and to the right, with each one of your moves you can imagine dropping down

00:16:27.382 --> 00:16:31.736
one more of these arcs, one more segment of circumference covering 2 theta radians

00:16:31.736 --> 00:16:32.260
of angle.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.738
It's not too hard to show that this condition of falling into the end zone,

00:16:36.738 --> 00:16:41.428
a region bound by this line perpendicular to all of our down and to the right lines,

00:16:41.428 --> 00:16:45.786
happens exactly when you've dropped so many arcs that if you tried to drop one

00:16:45.786 --> 00:16:50.200
more with the same length of 2 theta, you would necessarily cause some overlap.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:52.903
Essentially, it happens when you've filled up the

00:16:52.903 --> 00:16:55.480
circle as much as you can using pieces of this size.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:58.600
So how many arcs in total have we dropped down?

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:04.248
That's the same as asking how many times can you add some little value 2 theta to itself

00:17:04.248 --> 00:17:09.415
up until the point where adding one more would bust you over the total circumference of

00:17:09.415 --> 00:17:14.700
the circle, and that total circumference covers an angle of 360 degrees, or 2 pi radians.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.489
Phrasing things slightly differently than dividing out by that too,

00:17:18.489 --> 00:17:22.915
what is the biggest number you could multiply by this angle theta such that it stays

00:17:22.915 --> 00:17:23.800
smaller than pi?

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:29.178
For example, imagine we were in a situation so clean and beautiful that that

00:17:29.178 --> 00:17:33.940
little angle theta worked out to be exactly 0.01 as measured in radians.

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:38.954
The biggest number we could multiply it by would be 314, which gives us 3.14,

00:17:38.954 --> 00:17:43.170
and if we tried to bump up the number one more, that would be 3.15,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:44.720
which is bigger than pi.

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:49.270
And more generally, if that little angle is some small power of 10,

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:52.180
then this maximum number has the same digits as pi.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:54.720
I mean, this is what digits even mean.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:18:00.669
When you say pi is equal to 3.141, you can fit in 3,141 thousandths,

00:18:00.669 --> 00:18:04.460
and adding one more would bust you above pi.

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:08.740
At this point, maybe you're stepping back and saying, great, I see where this is going.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:11.836
In the case where the mass ratio is a power of 100,

00:18:11.836 --> 00:18:15.508
it's going to work out that that little angle is a clean power of 10,

00:18:15.508 --> 00:18:18.709
and because of all this state-space circle-puzzle reasoning,

00:18:18.709 --> 00:18:20.440
that will explain why we see pi.

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:24.093
This is almost true, but that's just not quite how it works out,

00:18:24.093 --> 00:18:26.888
and to make sure all of this is genuinely satisfying,

00:18:26.888 --> 00:18:29.580
let's take a moment to think through the last step.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:33.745
Remember how I said that the slope, not the angle, but the slope,

00:18:33.745 --> 00:18:36.660
looks like the negative square root of the mass ratio?

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:41.460
In our example where the mass ratio was 100 to 1, the slope looks like negative 10.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:43.340
What is slope?

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:44.380
What is that telling us?

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.571
It's the rise over run, which is the change in y

00:18:47.571 --> 00:18:50.780
divided by the change in x as you move along this line.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:53.880
How does that relate to that little angle I drew before?

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:57.631
The tangent of that angle, opposite over adjacent,

00:18:57.631 --> 00:19:01.420
is going to look like the change in x divided by the change in y.

00:19:01.700 --> 00:19:05.145
I'm putting a little negative sign in front of that y because in this diagram,

00:19:05.145 --> 00:19:07.840
that change in y is negative, so we want to counteract that.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.020
The tangent of this angle should be a positive number.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:12.495
This looks very similar to the slope, it's basically

00:19:12.495 --> 00:19:14.880
just flipping the expression and adding a negative sign.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:20.440
So the tangent of theta is equal to the square root of m2 divided by m1.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:25.140
So with a mass ratio of 100 to 1, the tangent of the angle is 0.1.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:29.560
This means that the angle itself would be the arctangent of 0.1.

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.740
So in other words, it's not quite the case that our angle

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:35.640
is so pretty and beautiful as being a small power of 10.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.740
It's instead the arctangent of a small power of 10.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:42.465
But if you go and graph the arctangent, you might notice that the

00:19:42.465 --> 00:19:45.880
arctangent of a small number is almost equal to that number itself.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:49.373
And the key idea is that for the purpose of our question,

00:19:49.373 --> 00:19:53.341
where we're saying what's the biggest whole number that you can multiply by this

00:19:53.341 --> 00:19:55.644
little angle that keeps it from surpassing pi,

00:19:55.644 --> 00:19:58.780
these numbers are so close that they might as well be the same.

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:00.560
You get the same whole number answer.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:04.376
In the example shown on screen, 3141 keeps you below pi,

00:20:04.376 --> 00:20:07.040
but adding one more would bust you above pi.

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:11.139
This idea that the arctangent of a small number is the same as the number

00:20:11.139 --> 00:20:14.499
itself is equivalent to saying the tangent of a small number is close to

00:20:14.499 --> 00:20:17.720
that number itself, and this is known as a small angle approximation.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:21.323
There is an international law saying that when you're doing physics for

00:20:21.323 --> 00:20:24.580
more than a half hour, you must use at least one small angle approximation.

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:26.850
And if you've never seen these before, there's a really

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:29.000
nice geometric reason why you would expect this to be true.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:32.729
If you think about a circle with a radius 1, a unit circle,

00:20:32.729 --> 00:20:36.297
and you consider a point an angle theta off the horizontal,

00:20:36.297 --> 00:20:40.877
what the tangent of that angle is telling you is the y-coordinate divided by

00:20:40.877 --> 00:20:42.780
the x-coordinate of that point.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:47.688
If your angle is really small, then that x-coordinate is basically the radius,

00:20:47.688 --> 00:20:51.887
it's basically just 1, and that y-coordinate is really close to the arc

00:20:51.887 --> 00:20:56.260
length along the circle between the x-axis and the point we're looking at.

00:20:56.620 --> 00:21:00.080
And since it's a unit circle, that arc length is the angle, it is theta.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:03.978
So rolling everything back, using a small angle approximation,

00:21:03.978 --> 00:21:07.584
this critical value theta we care about is close enough to a power

00:21:07.584 --> 00:21:11.460
of 10 that it explains why our answer would have the same digits as pi.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:15.085
That in turn explains why the number of lines we draw

00:21:15.085 --> 00:21:18.100
in the circle diagram must have the same digits as pi.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:23.240
And this circle diagram arose from a certain state space describing the colliding blocks.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:28.098
Where at a very high level, if you want to say why did pi have anything to do with this,

00:21:28.098 --> 00:21:31.539
it's because the conservation of energy for a two-body system like

00:21:31.539 --> 00:21:34.260
this simply looks like a circle in that state space.

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:37.534
But a few sticklers could be raising their hands and saying,

00:21:37.534 --> 00:21:41.080
are we positive that small angle approximations are enough here?

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:43.734
Can we be absolutely sure that there's not going to be

00:21:43.734 --> 00:21:46.260
a little off by one error because of the deviation here?

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:50.755
And for any calculus students among you, the more rigorous way to do this analysis would

00:21:50.755 --> 00:21:53.904
be to pull up the Taylor's approximation for the tangent of theta,

00:21:53.904 --> 00:21:58.086
where you can see that the error between the tangent of theta and theta itself is on the

00:21:58.086 --> 00:21:59.120
order of theta cubed.

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:02.260
So for example, if theta was a number like 1 one hundredth,

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:05.593
then when you approximate the tangent of this value as being just

00:22:05.593 --> 00:22:09.380
1 one hundredth itself, the error term is on the order of 1 one millionth.

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:11.960
Now that's very small, and so as you multiply by this,

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.040
that error doesn't really accumulate very much.

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:18.237
But it is possible for the final answer to our question to be an integer whose

00:22:18.237 --> 00:22:22.320
digits aren't quite the same as pi but off by one if the following was true.

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:26.296
If at any point when you're looking at all the digits of pi and you

00:22:26.296 --> 00:22:29.976
consider the first n of those digits, if it ever happens that the

00:22:29.976 --> 00:22:33.600
next n digits are all nines, then you get this off by one error.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:36.900
For all of the digits of pi that we know, this is not true.

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:41.182
Considering how many we know, and therefore how many nines you'd have to see in a row,

00:22:41.182 --> 00:22:44.300
it seems absurdly unlikely that something like this would be true.

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:48.260
However, rigorously proving that this is true of the digits of pi

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:51.800
is beyond the scope of what the current tools of math can prove.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:56.640
So very technically speaking, this fact that colliding blocks with a mass

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:01.140
ratio that's a power of 100 can compute pi for you is an unsolved problem.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:03.610
Of course, the block collisions themselves are

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:05.500
something you and I now understand very well.

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:08.640
You can if you want write an exact formula to answer it.

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:11.795
And if you like to pause and ponder, you might enjoy taking a moment

00:23:11.795 --> 00:23:14.500
to think about how there's nothing special about powers of 100.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.628
If you were working in another base, like base 2,

00:23:17.628 --> 00:23:21.971
then mass ratios with a power of 4 would count up to something that has the same

00:23:21.971 --> 00:23:23.580
bits as pi written in binary.

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:28.220
Now before you leave, it would be a shame to finish all this

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:31.140
without highlighting why this puzzle is worth studying at all.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:34.700
I mentioned already how Matt Parker's Pi Day video this year is all

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:37.418
about putting this block collision process into practice,

00:23:37.418 --> 00:23:41.120
and suffice to say, you run into practical issues very quickly in the attempt.

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:44.264
So some of you might complain, you know, is everything that

00:23:44.264 --> 00:23:47.040
we just did over-idealized to the point of pointlessness?

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:51.077
And there's two justifications I could give you for stripping away the messiness of the

00:23:51.077 --> 00:23:54.054
real world, one that's straightforward and another that's a little more

00:23:54.054 --> 00:23:54.840
thought-provoking.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.552
The first is that if you want to solve any problem,

00:23:57.552 --> 00:24:00.604
it's often helpful to start with the simplest possible variant,

00:24:00.604 --> 00:24:04.514
and then once your teeth are sunk into that, you often have a first approximation

00:24:04.514 --> 00:24:08.233
of reality, and very often when you want to account for the added details and

00:24:08.233 --> 00:24:12.048
messiness of the real world, the way that works is to start with the simplistic

00:24:12.048 --> 00:24:15.100
solution and slowly modify it step by step to be more accurate.

00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:19.451
Here for example, if you want to take into account the energy loss in each collision,

00:24:19.451 --> 00:24:22.940
that might look like having the circle shrink with each one of those clacks.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:27.383
Now the deeper reason for abstracting away from the messiness

00:24:27.383 --> 00:24:30.680
of the real world is that purity can expose hidden connections.

00:24:31.100 --> 00:24:34.863
This whole video is already about one hidden connection between blocks and pi,

00:24:34.863 --> 00:24:38.916
but the original paper that described this phenomenon by Gregory Galperin presented

00:24:38.916 --> 00:24:42.728
it in the context of yet another connection, a beautiful analogy between these

00:24:42.728 --> 00:24:46.733
colliding blocks and the way that a beam of light would bounce between two mirrors

00:24:46.733 --> 00:24:47.360
at an angle.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:50.037
If you want the details, another one of the videos

00:24:50.037 --> 00:24:52.400
I made back in 2019 is all about that perspective.

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:55.480
If you liked this, you will almost certainly enjoy that video.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:58.981
But even more surprising is what I referenced at the beginning,

00:24:58.981 --> 00:25:02.919
how this whole solution is secretly mirrored inside an algorithm within quantum

00:25:02.919 --> 00:25:03.460
computing.

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.890
There's a lot to unpack to explain what that is,

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:09.105
which is why I'm pulling that out into a separate video that we'll do next,

00:25:09.105 --> 00:25:11.600
but for right now I just want to leave you with one point.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:15.847
When pure mathematicians pull out the messiness of reality from their problems,

00:25:15.847 --> 00:25:17.420
it's not just out of laziness.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.165
I mean it's a little bit laziness, but it's not just that,

00:25:20.165 --> 00:25:22.000
and it's not out of apathy for applications.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:26.895
It's because distilling a problem into its core essence can expose hidden connections,

00:25:26.895 --> 00:25:30.880
and it's through those connections that mathematicians make progress.

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:40.123
A hard problem in one context, where it might seem deeply confusing,

00:25:40.123 --> 00:25:50.868
sometimes can look clearer in another setting, but the analogy may not be obvious,

00:25:50.868 --> 00:25:59.412
and a lot of the progress of math through history has looked like

00:25:59.412 --> 00:26:06.920
developing a richer and richer web of hidden connections.

